What:		/sys/bus/platform/drivers/vhci_hcd/nports
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	Jakub Lecki <lec.jakub@gmail.com>
Description:
		Read the total number of global ports as a decimal number with
		a trailing newline. Each configured controller contributes its
		high-speed and super-speed ports, including an unbound
		controller's unavailable ports. An empty pool reports zero.
		The mode is 0444. EAGAIN indicates pool replacement in progress.
		Controller zero retains a legacy nports file with the same value.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/drivers/vhci_hcd/attach
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	Jakub Lecki <lec.jakub@gmail.com>
Description:
		Write four whitespace-separated decimal numbers: global port,
		connected stream socket descriptor, remote device identifier
		and USB speed. The identifier combines the remote bus number
		in its upper 16 bits and device number in its lower 16 bits.
		Speeds use enum usb_device_speed: 1 low, 2 full, 3 high,
		4 wireless, 5 super and 6 super-plus. The port must belong to
		the matching high-speed or super-speed hub.

		The mode is 0200: only root may write. A successful write
		reports the byte count and retains a reference to the socket.
		EINVAL rejects malformed fields, an unsupported or mismatched
		speed, or an invalid socket. ENODEV identifies an unavailable
		port or unbound controller; EBUSY identifies an occupied port.
		EAGAIN indicates pool replacement in progress. Discovery does
		not reserve ports, so userspace must handle these races.
		Controller zero retains attach with the same format and behavior.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/drivers/vhci_hcd/detach
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	Jakub Lecki <lec.jakub@gmail.com>
Description:
		Write one decimal global port number to request disconnect.
		The mode is 0200: only root may write. A successful write
		reports the byte count; disconnect completes asynchronously.
		EINVAL rejects an invalid format, unavailable port or port that
		is already idle. EAGAIN indicates pool replacement in progress.
		Controller zero retains detach with the same behavior.

What:		/sys/devices/platform/vhci_hcd.N/status
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	Jakub Lecki <lec.jakub@gmail.com>
Description:
		Read-only (0444) status for controller N, also accessible through
		/sys/bus/platform/drivers/vhci_hcd/vhci_hcd.N/status. The header
		and columns match the legacy controller-zero status files:
		hub, global port, state, speed, remote device identifier,
		socket descriptor and local USB bus ID. Global ports are
		contiguous across the configured pool; a controller's high-speed
		ports precede its super-speed ports. ENODEV identifies an
		unbound controller. Each table fits in one sysfs page.
		The header names are hub, port, sta, spd, dev, sockfd and
		local_busid. Hubs are hs or ss. States are 4 idle, 5 not yet
		assigned, 6 in use and 7 error. Speeds use enum usb_device_speed.
		Ports, states, speeds and descriptors are decimal; the remote
		identifier is hexadecimal. An unused port reports zero device
		fields and local bus ID 0-0.
		Files are replaced when the pool is reconfigured; readers must
		handle ENOENT, ENODEV and EAGAIN and refresh discovery.

What:		/sys/devices/platform/vhci_hcd.N/num_ports
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	Jakub Lecki <lec.jakub@gmail.com>
Description:
		Read-only decimal ports-per-hub for a bound controller. The
		value is shared by all controllers and remains fixed until
		reconfiguration. Global port numbers are provided by status.

What:		/sys/devices/platform/vhci_hcd.0/nports
What:		/sys/devices/platform/vhci_hcd.0/attach
What:		/sys/devices/platform/vhci_hcd.0/detach
What:		/sys/devices/platform/vhci_hcd.0/status.N
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	Jakub Lecki <lec.jakub@gmail.com>
Description:
		Silent compatibility interfaces for older USB/IP tools, present
		on bound controller zero. nports, attach and detach use the same
		formats, permissions and global ports as the driver attributes.
		status.N names controllers N greater than zero and matches that
		controller's status table; an unbound controller emits unassigned
		rows. Current tools read status directly from each controller.

		These interfaces emit no deprecation warnings and have no
		scheduled removal. Controller zero's own status file is shared
		by original and updated tools.

		Original tools work only within their existing 8-bit port limit;
		ports above 255 require updated tools. A status read does not
		reserve a port: attach may report ENODEV, EBUSY or EINVAL if the
		pool or the port's hub changed.
